<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Clare</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County Clare is a county in the west of Ireland, between the Shannon estuary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galway">Galway</a> Bay. It was formed from the Gaelic kingdom of Thomond, ruled by the O'Briens, when the English administration shired the area in the 1570s. The O'Brien earls of Thomond, who held their seat at Bunratty, remained its leading family.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/county-clare.jpg" alt="County Clare" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:County_Clare_Coat_of_Arms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>County Clare lies in the province of Munster, bordered on the west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-shannon">River Shannon</a> and the Shannon Estuary lie to the southeast and south, Lough Derg to the northeast and Galway Bay to the north. It covers 3,450 square kilometres. The county contains the Burren, a karst region with rare flowers and fauna, and at its western edge the Cliffs of Moher. Its nickname is the Banner County, for which two origins are suggested: the banners captured by Clare's Dragoons at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ramillies">Battle of Ramillies</a>, and the banner of Catholic emancipation raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-oconnell">Daniel O'Connell</a>'s victory in the 1828 by-election.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Clare holds the oldest known evidence of human activity in Ireland: a bear's patella with butchery marks, found in the Alice and Gwendoline Cave near Clarecastle and dated to about 10,500 BCE. It is also one of the richest places in Ireland for dolmens, the best known being Poulnabrone in the Burren, where remains have been dated to 3800 BCE. In the Early Middle Ages the area was part of the Kingdom of Connacht. It was then annexed to Munster, settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-cais">Dalcassians</a> and renamed Thomond, meaning North Munster, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a> rose to leadership from there. From 1118 the Kingdom of Thomond was a petty kingdom ruled by the O'Brien clan.</p><p>After the Norman invasion Thomas de Clare established a short-lived Norman lordship of Thomond, and his son Richard de Clare was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dysert-odea">Battle of Dysert O'Dea</a> in 1318. In 1543 Murrough O'Brien became Earl of Thomond by surrender and regrant to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>. The Lord Deputy Henry Sidney transferred Thomond from Munster to Connaught and shired it, so that Thomond became County Clare. The name is explained either from Thomas de Clare's Castle of Clare, after which the county was named in 1590, or from the settlement of Clare, now Clarecastle, whose Irish name Clár means a plank bridge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The county seat and largest settlement is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennis">Ennis</a>, and Clare County Council is the local authority. The population was 127,938 at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Clare is known for traditional music, among it the Willie Clancy Summer School at Milltown Malbay. Shannon Airport, which opened in 1945, was the first in Ireland to receive transatlantic flights. Bunratty Castle and the Cliffs of Moher are among its places of interest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “County Clare” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was forced through by Wellington and Peel after Daniel O'Connell's election in <b>County Clare</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 places the Gangani on the west coast of Ireland, south of the Auteini and north of the Velabri, usually located in <b>County Clare</b> or around the mouth of the Shannon.</blockquote><a href="/state/gangani-ireland">Gangani (Ireland)</a></li><li><blockquote>Thomond arose from the Dal Cais lands in <b>Clare</b> and Limerick.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-thomond">Kingdom of Thomond</a></li></ul></section>
